explanation:
The definition is just `PresentedGroup.of (p, a)`: it sends the generator (p, a) to its class in the group defined by the presentation. That presentation uses the standard Steinberg relations over an arbitrary ring R:
- additivity: x_p(a) x_p(b) = x_p(a+b);
- [x_ij(a), x_kl(b)] = 1 when j ≠ k and i ≠ l;
- [x_ij(a), x_jk(b)] = x_ik(ab) when i ≠ k.

Mathlib's commutator convention ⁅g,h⁆ = g h g⁻¹ h⁻¹ makes the third relation agree with the textbook form. The generality is the natural one: any index type and any (not necessarily commutative) ring. This is exactly the canonical symbol x_ij(a) of the Steinberg group. Mathlib has no Steinberg group, so there is no counterpart.
